            <div id="l9mku4v">
                  <section class="TextGray600Element body-font">
                        <div class="ContainerElement PaddingX5Element PaddingY24Element MarginXAutoElement">
                              <div class="FlexElement FlexColumnElement TextCenterElement WidthFullElement MarginBottom20Element">
                                    <h1 class="SmallText3XLElement Text2XLElement FontMediumElement title-font TextGray900Element">Design choices that work together</h1>
                                    <p class="LargeWidthTwoThirdElement MarginXAutoElement LeadingRelaxedElement TextBaseElement">
                                          A well-considered interior is more than a collection of attractive objects. It is a relationship between the people who live there, the way rooms connect and the details that give a home its character. Blueprint Living's design perspective brings those considerations into one conversation, helping establish priorities before choices are made. The result is a clearer framework for thinking about space, atmosphere and practical needs, while leaving room
                                          for personal taste and the realities of each individual home.
                                    </p>
                              </div>
                              <div class="FlexElement FlexWrapElement NegativeMargin4Element">
                                    <div class="Padding4Element MediumWidthHalfElement LargeWidthThirdElement WidthFullElement">
                                          <div class="FlexElement RoundedLargeElement HeightFullElement BGGray100Element Padding8Element FlexColumnElement">
                                                <div class="FlexElement ItemsCenterElement MarginBottom3Element">
                                                      <h2 class="TextGray900Element TextLargeElement title-font FontMediumElement">Start with the way you live</h2>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="FlexGrowElement">
                                                      <p class="LeadingRelaxedElement TextBaseElement">
                                                            Every home has its own routines, constraints and sources of inspiration. A useful design conversation starts by considering how each room is used, what already works and what feels difficult. That context can help guide decisions about layout, storage, furniture and the balance between shared and private spaces. Rather than treating a room as a blank canvas, this approach takes existing habits and belongings into account, so design
                                                            ideas have a meaningful connection to everyday life. The right priorities will differ from one household to another.
                                                      </p>
                                                </div>
                                          </div>
                                    </div>
                                    <div class="Padding4Element MediumWidthHalfElement LargeWidthThirdElement WidthFullElement">
                                          <div class="FlexElement RoundedLargeElement HeightFullElement BGGray100Element Padding8Element FlexColumnElement">
                                                <div class="FlexElement ItemsCenterElement MarginBottom3Element">
                                                      <h2 class="TextGray900Element TextLargeElement title-font FontMediumElement">Build a coherent visual direction</h2>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="FlexGrowElement">
                                                      <p class="LeadingRelaxedElement TextBaseElement">
                                                            Colour, materials, finishes and furnishings can each make a room feel different, but they are most effective when considered in relation to one another. Developing a visual direction gives those choices a common thread while allowing rooms to retain their own character. It can help clarify whether a palette should feel calm, expressive, warm or restrained, and how texture and contrast might support that feeling. This is a framework for
                                                            making decisions, not a fixed formula, and it should reflect the preferences and practical needs of the people who live in the home.
                                                      </p>
                                                </div>
                                          </div>
                                    </div>
                                    <div class="Padding4Element MediumWidthHalfElement LargeWidthThirdElement WidthFullElement">
                                          <div class="FlexElement RoundedLargeElement HeightFullElement BGGray100Element Padding8Element FlexColumnElement">
                                                <div class="FlexElement ItemsCenterElement MarginBottom3Element">
                                                      <h2 class="TextGray900Element TextLargeElement title-font FontMediumElement">Give space and light careful attention</h2>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="FlexGrowElement">
                                                      <p class="LeadingRelaxedElement TextBaseElement">
                                                            The proportions of a room, the position of its openings and the quality of available light all influence how an interior is experienced. Considering these elements early can help inform furniture placement, colour choices and the use of different zones. A thoughtful plan looks at how people move through the space as well as how it appears in photographs, and recognises that natural light changes over the course of a day. Every home is
                                                            different, so ideas should be considered in the context of its actual layout and conditions rather than assumed to work everywhere.
                                                      </p>
                                                </div>
                                          </div>
                                    </div>
                                    <div class="Padding4Element MediumWidthHalfElement LargeWidthThirdElement WidthFullElement MarginXAutoElement">
                                          <div class="FlexElement RoundedLargeElement HeightFullElement BGGray100Element Padding8Element FlexColumnElement">
                                                <div class="FlexElement ItemsCenterElement MarginBottom3Element">
                                                      <h2 class="TextGray900Element TextLargeElement title-font FontMediumElement">Keep personal character in the plan</h2>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="FlexGrowElement">
                                                      <p class="LeadingRelaxedElement TextBaseElement">
                                                            A home should feel connected to the people who inhabit it, not simply arranged around a passing trend. Personal objects, favourite colours, artwork and meaningful materials can all help give a space a sense of identity. A considered design direction makes room for these elements and looks for ways they can sit comfortably alongside newer choices. The aim is a balanced result that feels intentional without becoming impersonal.
                                                            Preferences can evolve, so a flexible approach can also leave space for future changes as needs and tastes develop.
                                                      </p>
                                                </div>
                                          </div>
                                    </div>
                              </div>
                        </div>
